// Ported from docs/design/_adherence.oxlintrc.json. Run with
// `npx oxlint --import-plugin` — no-restricted-imports lives in the "import"
// plugin, which oxlint only activates when that flag is passed (declaring
// "import" under `plugins` above isn't enough on its own). See Notes.md,
// "What the bundle gave and didn't give," for the full reasoning behind what
// changed on the way in:
//
// 1. no-restricted-imports' `group` glob updated from the source bundle's
//    "components/<folder>/**" (a docs/design-relative path) to
//    "@/components/design-system/*/**" (this project's real import alias) —
//    the original glob would never have matched anything real code actually
//    imports. Verified live: a deliberate violating import correctly warns
//    under this config.
// 2. The index-file override glob updated from "**/index.js" to
//    "**/design-system/index.ts" to match the real barrel file, and scoped to
//    the design-system folder specifically (src/components/patterns has no
//    such barrel/exemption).
// 3. Dropped entirely: the source config's `no-restricted-syntax` rule — both
//    its 41 per-component "doesn't accept that prop" selectors and the 3
//    raw-hex/px/font-family bans, plus the "x-omelette" metadata block that
//    generated the former. oxlint 1.79 (the version this project installs)
//    does not implement no-restricted-syntax at all: `oxlint -c
//    _adherence.oxlintrc.json` fails outright with "Rule 'no-restricted-syntax'
//    not found in plugin 'eslint'" — the source bundle's config was written
//    against ESLint's rule catalog, not oxlint's actual (smaller, Rust-native)
//    one, so this half of it was never actually runnable. Separately, even if
//    it were supported, the 41 prop-contract selectors would have been
//    redundant with TypeScript now that every component is a real .tsx file
//    with a real interface (Badge's `status` literal union already rejects a
//    bogus value at compile time) — and actively wrong for the six components
//    that spread `{...rest}` onto a native element and extend React's own
//    *HTMLAttributes<...> (Button, IconButton, Input, Textarea, Select,
//    Checkbox): the generated allowlist only included each component's own
//    custom props, so it would have flagged ordinary correct usage like
//    `<Input type="email" value={x} onChange={...} />` as a violation.
{
  "plugins": ["react", "import"],
  "rules": {
    "no-restricted-imports": [
      "warn",
      {
        "patterns": [
          {
            "group": ["@/components/design-system/*/**"],
            "message": "Import design-system components from '@/components/design-system' (the barrel), not component internals."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "overrides": [
    {
      "files": ["**/design-system/index.ts"],
      "rules": {
        "no-restricted-imports": "off"
      }
    }
  ]
}
